From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: rzxvx <rrzxznewid@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 06:25:40 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 21/21] mtd: apple-sftl: Give up a block whose program fails

A failed program stops the append writer and, with H2FMI, every later
I/O on the bus. Cascadia's writer instead gives the superblock up for
the session and carries on, and that path ran on a 16 GB P105 under
injected program and erase failures with iOS clean afterwards.

Port it into the append writer. On a failed data or summary program:

- program nothing more into the block and do not close it;
- move every logical page the map still places there to a new append
  block with a fresh sequence, keeping the record type and, for
  relocated user data, the user epoch, as reclamation does; the failed
  request follows with the highest sequences;
- a failed page that reads back erased consumed nothing, so its
  sequences are offered again and the journal stays contiguous; one
  that reads back exactly as programmed keeps its records and
  sequences;
- a further failure gives up the new block as well and the move starts
  over from the map, up to eight failures per session; without blank
  blocks the request returns -ENOSPC and the move resumes with the next
  request.

A torn page still stops the writer, because a fresh load rejects it;
the next patch handles torn pages at load. The VFL is never touched: a
really bad block meets its next failure under iOS, which retires it.
Relocation inside reclamation keeps its own stop-on-failure rule.

Cascadia also records the given-up superblock as awaiting erase with
nothing valid in its next context commit. The append writer writes no
checkpoint on the P105, so the block stays an open block on the medium
whose records are all superseded.

The KUnit tests that asserted the old stop-on-failure behaviour now
assert the give-up: a blank failed page, a block with live pages, a
failure on every block until no blank block is left, a page that landed
despite a failed completion, and failed summary pages that move all 480
pages of a full block.

Ported from Cascadia (https://github.com/rzxvx/Project-Cascadia) commit
c2958e1 ("a5: FTL writer: survive program and erase failures (spec
4.9)"), file sftl_write.c (mark_bad, take_spare, frontier_fail) and
devices/a5/docs/research/p105-nand-spec.md section 4.9, as of Cascadia
ad28ba4.

Assisted-by: LLM
